1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:12,414
Every May, Eurovision sparks the same argument: is it the song, or is it politics? The problem: points mix song quality with voting bias. We separate the two, and forecast the result.

2
00:00:12,964 --> 00:00:27,244
The data was messy: nearly two thousand nested files, voting rules that changed twice, and five country-code systems. We normalised every vote to one scale, mapped every code, and checked that every score adds up.

3
00:00:27,794 --> 00:00:38,642
Our pipeline runs end to end with one command: ingest, clean, build features from past years only, model, calibrate and test, then publish to the dashboard.

4
00:00:39,192 --> 00:00:52,056
The core idea: compare each vote with what every other country gave the same song. Lithuania gives Latvia over 9 points out of twelve, while everyone else gives about 2. That gap is bias, and it runs both ways.

5
00:00:52,606 --> 00:01:06,046
Each question gets a fitting model: clustering for voting blocs, regression with bootstrap intervals for geography and diaspora, a ridge regression that predicts every country's points to every entry, and logistic calibration for probabilities.

6
00:01:06,596 --> 00:01:16,052
The findings: voting ties are stable across decades, the televote follows migrant communities while juries barely do, and hosts and the Big Five underperform.

7
00:01:16,602 --> 00:01:28,962
We compared alternatives on unseen years. Voting affinity lifts pair-level accuracy from point 28 to point 48. Gradient boosting was no more accurate, so we kept the explainable ridge model.

8
00:01:29,512 --> 00:01:40,888
On performance: after the semi-finals, rank correlation doubles to point 66, the winner is in our top three in 11 of 14 finals, and we ranked the withheld 2026 winner first.

9
00:01:41,438 --> 00:01:50,630
It is sustainable: open data, a one-command rebuild every year, and clear limits. Before the semi-finals, no one can judge a new song.

10
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:54,814
Eurovision isn't random. It's measurable.
